/**
 * Transient and persistent highlights — §8.7.
 *
 * Two things share one mechanism. A grid can flash a cell when its value
 * changes, and a host can call `grid.highlight(...)` to mark a cell, a row or a
 * column for as long as it likes. Both are "paint this target in this colour
 * until this moment", so both are one record here.
 *
 * The model holds *what* is highlighted, never any DOM. That matters more than
 * it sounds: rows are recycled as the user scrolls, so a highlight written
 * directly onto an element would be wiped by the next repaint and reappear on
 * whatever unrelated row inherited that element. The renderer asks this model
 * on every paint instead, which makes a highlight survive scrolling, sorting
 * and filtering without any of them knowing it exists.
 *
 * Expiry is read, not scheduled. A timer per highlight on a grid flashing
 * hundreds of cells a second is a great deal of bookkeeping to arrive at a
 * question that can be answered by comparing two numbers; one timer wakes the
 * renderer when the earliest highlight is due to end, and that is all.
 *
 * No DOM.
 */



/** What a highlight can be attached to. */
