/**
 * Cell pool — §7.3.
 *
 * "Cell reuse requires matching column and renderer type, otherwise the pool
 * allocates." That sentence is the whole design: buckets are keyed by
 * `columnId` **and** renderer kind, so a recycled row hands its `price` cell
 * back and the incoming row takes the same node for its own `price` cell. The
 * node keeps its classes, its compiled template's text-node references and any
 * component instance the cell layer attached to it, so the update is a
 * `textContent` write and nothing else.
 *
 * A cell whose column or renderer kind does not match is not repurposed —
 * doing so would mean tearing down and rebuilding whatever the cell layer put
 * inside it, which costs more than a fresh element.
 */

/** Free nodes kept per bucket. Beyond this, released cells are dropped. */
const DEFAULT_BUCKET_MAX = 32;

/**
 * @typedef {object} CellRecord
 * @property {object} el the cell element
 * @property {string} columnId column the node is built for
 * @property {string} kind renderer kind the node is built for
 * @property {unknown} state scratch space owned by the cell layer
 * @property {string} text last text written, so an unchanged cell is skipped
 */

/**
 * A pool of cell elements, bucketed by column and renderer kind.
 */
